1. Yann can choose any of the 10 items on the menu.
2. For each choice Camille makes, Camille has 9 remaining choices (since Camille cannot choose the same dish as Yann).

Thus, the total number of different combinations of meals is the number of choices Yann has multiplied by the number of choices Camille has after Yann makes his choice. This can be calculated as:

\[ 10 \times 9 = 90 \]

</think>

